  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "A projectile is projected vertically downward from a height of $294\\,\\text{m}$ with an initial velocity of $19.6\\,\\text{m/s}.$ How long will it take for the projectile to hit the ground?\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*\n\nThe projectile hits the ground in{{field-1}}seconds.",
  "worked_solution": "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the projectile is $u=19.6\\,\\text{m/s}.$\n\n- The projectile covers the distance of $s=294\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $t,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(294) & =(19.6)t+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (9.8){t}^{2} \\\\ 294 & =19.6t+4.9{t}^{2} \\\\ 60(4.9) & =4(4.9)t+4.9{t}^{2} \\\\ 60\\cancel{(4.9)} & =4\\cancel{(4.9)}t+\\cancel{(4.9)}{t}^{2} \\\\ 60 & =4t+{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+4t-60 & =0 \\\\ (t+10)(t-6) & =0 \\\\ t & =-10,\\,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution. Therefore, the projectile hits the ground after $t=6$ seconds.",
